    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p20-q13-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find $x$ in terms of $c$, given that $\\dfrac{2}{3x-2c}+\\dfrac{3}{2x-3c}=\\dfrac{7}{2c}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=2c$ or $x=\\dfrac{11c}{14}$, where $c\\ne0$.\n\nClear the denominators and factorise the resulting quadratic.",
          "worked": "The denominators require $c\\ne0$, $x\\ne\\dfrac{2c}{3}$ and $x\\ne\\dfrac{3c}{2}$.\n\nMultiplying by $2c(3x-2c)(2x-3c)$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}4c(2x-3c)+6c(3x-2c)&=7(3x-2c)(2x-3c)\\\\26cx-24c^2&=42x^2-91cx+42c^2\\\\42x^2-117cx+66c^2&=0\\\\14x^2-39cx+22c^2&=0\\\\(x-2c)(14x-11c)&=0\\\\x&=2c\\text{ or }\\frac{11c}{14}.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth values satisfy the denominator restrictions when $c\\ne0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 66
      },
      {
        "id": "p20-q13-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find $x$ in terms of $a$ and $b$ if $\\dfrac{a^2b}{x^2}+\\left(1+\\dfrac{b}{x}\\right)a=2b+\\dfrac{a^2}{x}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Take the nonzero values among $x=a$ and $x=\\dfrac{ab}{a-2b}$; the second expression applies when $a\\ne2b$.\n\nIf $a=b=0$, every $x\\ne0$ is a solution. If $a=0$ and $b\\ne0$, there is no solution.\n\nMultiply by $x^2$ and factorise.",
          "worked": "The original equation requires $x\\ne0$. Multiplying by $x^2$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2b+ax^2+abx&=2bx^2+a^2x\\\\(a-2b)x^2+(ab-a^2)x+a^2b&=0\\\\(x-a)\\bigl((a-2b)x-ab\\bigr)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $x=a$ is a solution when $a\\ne0$. When $a\\ne2b$, the other candidate is\n$$\\begin{align*}(a-2b)x&=ab\\\\x&=\\frac{ab}{a-2b},\\end{align*}$$\nand it is retained only if it is nonzero.\n\nIf $a=2b\\ne0$, the second factor is the nonzero constant $-ab$, so $x=a$ is the only solution. If $a=b=0$, the original equation is $0=0$ for every $x\\ne0$. If $a=0$ and $b\\ne0$, it becomes $0=2b$, so there is no solution.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 82
      }
    ]
  },
